<!--Post: 2716--><!--Time: 1104010972-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2716"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Victoria<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Victoria<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, December 25, 2004 - 1:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Where would a killer keep mementos for decades without fear that any thing, except death, could expose them? Any ideas? I suggest looking at SDB kept by males for decades.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2716-->
<!--Post: 2719--><!--Time: 1104023247-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2719"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, December 25, 2004 - 5:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Good thought Victoria, but, if i&#39;m not mistaken, a warrant is needed for that.  Safety deposit boxes are EXTREMELY priviledged.  Sufficient evidence would be needed for ONE bank; let alone ALL banks.  So, let&#39;s find some evidence.  I like that theory.  So obvious, yet I don&#39;t believe ever spoken about.  Good work!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2719-->
<!--Post: 2734--><!--Time: 1104037250-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2734"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, December 25, 2004 - 9:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I predict BTK has the items at his residence and that they are unbelievably well-hidden. But I love <BR>the safety deposit box idea. It&#39;s very solid. The thing is, BTK trusts <b>nobody</b> and that may include any bank. BTK also probably uses the mementos as fantasy fodder and that points toward keeping things at home. But Victoria you&#39;ve given me a great idea, thank you!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2734-->
<!--Post: 2735--><!--Time: 1104037536-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2735"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dawgar<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dawgar<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, December 25, 2004 - 9:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->good idea victoria <BR>but i think george is correct about the mementos and that he probably doesnt trust anything or person.. <BR>having said that...this guy isnt typical so the bank idea is possible<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2735-->
<!--Post: 2757--><!--Time: 1104080333-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2757"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Victoria<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Victoria<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 26, 2004 - 8:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My first post, so thanks for the encouragment. SDV contents are privileged, but do you think the police could get a list of people who have kept SDB for decades?  <BR> <BR>I just can&#39;t imagine where he would keep the evidence. In his house? Buried in the back yard?Wouldn&#39;t that mean he could never leave home? 25 years is a long time without a vacation or illness. Too risky. Any ideas on hiding places?  <BR> <BR>As far as SDB you have your own little room and can stay as long as you like. Sure, police need a warrant to go inside a box, but would they need one to take a look at a list of owners?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2757-->
<!--Post: 2802--><!--Time: 1104158823-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2802"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 6:47 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Letters Testamentary will get one in as well. Of course that will only help if BTK is dead. He could have a room in a storage facility as well. They are very respectful of privacy at those places. All he has to do is make his payments; and if he is wise he will have paid years in advance. If BTK is a do-it-yourselfer, one would have to dismantle his residence to find things that he has hidden. One can also hid things within a vehicle, boat, RV, hangar, airplane &#40;except at annual time&#41;, etc.  Whatever and wherever he has his mementos, you can be assured that he has prepared for every scenario including his demise, whether natural, accidental, etc. If he is a  traveler, he may have his stash in any number of places. Only he can put all the puzzle pieces together. Dean Coryll used a stoarge facility to hide bodies. No one even complained about any odor. <BR> <BR>Of course, there was the man who stored his deceased wife&#39;s body at his brother&#39;s home for quite awhile. Eventually they got tired of it and curious and opened it and found the mummified remains. I do not think that BTK is that stupid, however.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2802-->
<!--Post: 3047--><!--Time: 1104380944-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3047"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, December 29, 2004 - 8:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Victoria...  I do not believe a warrant is needed to see a list of names of SDB renters, but, keep in mind, that the police would find a lot of renters that have had them for 25&#43; years.  I have found that the elderly DO NOT like change and will keep their SDB until death.  Also, as far as the privacy issue, TWO keys are needed to open the box; the master that the bank manager keeps and the key the customer keeps.  If the customer&#39;s key is lost, the bank charges a huge fee to cut open the box as that is the only other way to open it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3047-->
<!--Post: 3050--><!--Time: 1104382711-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3050"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Secret_squirrel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Secret_squirrel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, December 29, 2004 - 8:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Im only curious Dee, but what happens if a person dies and payment obviously ceases.  Is there a statute of limitations allowing the bank access to its contents? I would think B would want all his momentos close at hand to look at from time to time.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3050-->
<!--Post: 3067--><!--Time: 1104417827-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3067"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 6:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->squirrel...  In the event of death, the SDB becomes the ward of the &#34;state&#34;.  I put state in quotes because if there is no will, the contents get rolled up in the whole probate mess.  If there is a will, the contents automatically become property of the heir; unless otherwise specified in the will.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3067-->
</table></td></tr></table>
